{
  "requirement": "The `{term}` role followed by a backtick-delimited full definition name MUST reference a named definition. In rendered Markdown, the reference MUST display the definition's phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to its anchored declaration, without a badge. The phrase MUST be escaped appropriately for rendering.\n\nMissing targets and targets of another kind MUST be reported. Escaped references and references in Markdown code examples MUST remain literal. A term reference MUST NOT satisfy a requirement citation or a Rust function's requirement-coverage obligation.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is satisfied.** Each obligation has implementing code marked `{impl}`, and tests cover the main cases. The gaps I found are minor.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **`{term}` recognition.** `reference_roles` in `src/lib.rs` recognises `{term}` followed by a backtick-delimited name and requires `valid_name` (full dotted names). It skips escaped matches via `is_escaped` and, in Markdown, code spans, code blocks and inline HTML via `protected_ranges`/`is_protected`. That keeps escaped and code-example references literal.\n- **Rendering.** `replace_inline_references` in `src/lib.rs` resolves a term reference only when the target is a `DefinitionKind::Term` whose declaration parses.\n  - It emits a bare `<a href=\"…#habeas-definition-<name>\">phrase</a>`, with no class and no badge.\n  - The phrase goes through `terms::escaped_phrase`, which escapes Markdown specials as numeric entities and HTML specials via `escape_html`.\n  - The link target is the anchored declaration. `replace_definitions` emits `<strong id=\"habeas-definition-…\">` using `DefinitionKind::anchor`.\n  - An unresolved or wrong-kind term stays literal.\n- **Error reporting.** `validate_references` in `src/evidence.rs` reports a `{term}` whose target is missing or of another kind as \"term X has no matching named definition\". It then `continue`s, so a term reference never enters `by_definition` and cannot satisfy requirement citation or evidence coverage. For `{impl}`, `{cite}` and `{test}`, a Term target is reported as \"has no matching requirement or example\".\n- **Rust coverage.** `missing_function_references` in `src/rust_coverage.rs` returns `false` for `ReferenceKind::Term` in `counts`, and treats a Term-kind definition as not counting. A term therefore never satisfies a Rust function's coverage obligation.\n- **Supporting code.** `ReferenceKind`, `label` and `color` carry the Term variant. `referenced_terms` in `src/terms.rs` feeds term dependencies. All are marked `{impl}`.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` covers a term pointing at a requirement (wrong kind) and a missing term, checking the exact error text.\n- `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` covers:\n  - exactly one rendered hyperlink, even though the source also has an inline-code reference, a fenced reference and an escaped reference;\n  - no shields.io badge;\n  - the phrase `*alpha* & <beta>` rendered as `&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;`;\n  - the declaration anchor.\n- `a_term_reference_renders_only_its_phrase_as_a_plain_link` (tagged `phrase_rendering`, not this requirement) asserts the whole output equals a single plain `<a href=…>phrase</a>`.\n- `definition_schema_deletion_and_literal_references_are_checked` shows literal term references in an inline code span, a fenced block and an escaped form produce no errors and no dependencies. It also shows a deleted term target is reported.\n- `outline_inputs_capture_terms_without_counting_them_as_citations` shows a function with only a `{term}` comment gets \"no requirement or example citation\".\n- The unit test `an_example_counts_only_when_it_references_a_requirement` in `src/rust_coverage.rs` confirms a term does not count for coverage. It is not tagged for this requirement.\n\n**Minor gaps, none enough to fail the requirement**\n\n- `replace_references` in `src/lib.rs` dispatches to the marked `replace_inline_references` but has no `{impl}` for this requirement itself. It carries the fenced-body recursion, so it is only an indirect dispatcher.\n- I did not find a test for an `{impl}`, `{cite}` or `{test}` reference that targets a term; it is \"another kind\" in the opposite direction. The code path is simple and shares its reporting with the missing-target case.\n\nThere are no `FIXME` acknowledgements.",
